# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Orialis Data Platform - environment template
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your own values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env          (macOS / Linux)
#     copy .env.example .env        (Windows)
#
# `.env` is ignored by Git and must NEVER be committed. This template carries
# placeholders only - no host, no password, no project reference.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PostgreSQL connection string for the Supabase-hosted CRM database.
#
# Shape:
#     postgresql://<USER>:<PASSWORD>@<HOST>:<PORT>/<DATABASE>?sslmode=require
#
# Where to find each value: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > Database >
# Connection string. The password is the one set when the project was created;
# it can be reset from that same page.
#
# Supabase exposes TWO endpoints, and the choice matters:
#
#   1. Connection pooler - port 6543, transaction mode (recommended for an API)
#      postgresql://postgres.<PROJECT_REF>:<PASSWORD>@aws-0-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?sslmode=require
#
#      Reachable over IPv4. Because the pooler runs in transaction mode it does
#      NOT support prepared statements, so psycopg must be created with
#      `prepare_threshold=None`. Session-scoped objects (temporary tables,
#      SET commands) only survive inside a single transaction.
#
#   2. Direct connection - port 5432 (simpler, better for migrations and seeds)
#      postgresql://postgres:<PASSWORD>@db.<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
#
#      Full session semantics, prepared statements allowed. On recent Supabase
#      projects this endpoint is IPv6-only unless the IPv4 add-on is enabled,
#      so it may be unreachable from some networks.
#
# `sslmode=require` matters on both endpoints. Supabase does NOT refuse
# plaintext - a connection made with `sslmode=disable` is accepted and runs
# unencrypted - and libpq defaults to `prefer`, which falls back to plaintext
# without saying so. If this parameter is missing, CRM/config.py appends
# `sslmode=require` so that every connection is encrypted; an sslmode you set
# yourself is never overwritten.
#
# If the password contains characters such as @ : / ? # or %, they must be
# percent-encoded, otherwise the URL is parsed incorrectly.

DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRM_API_KEY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Shared secret protecting every /api/v1 route. Callers send it in the
# `X-API-Key` header; requests without it, or with a wrong one, get 401.
#
# The API refuses to start if this is not set: an API that silently runs with
# authentication disabled is worse than one that will not boot.
#
# Generate one with:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#
# Use a different value per environment, and rotate it by replacing this line
# and restarting the application. 32+ random characters are recommended.

CRM_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ERP_DATABASE_URL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PostgreSQL connection string for the Orialis ERP - the SECOND source system.
#
# Shape (identical to DATABASE_URL above):
#     postgresql://<USER>:<PASSWORD>@<HOST>:<PORT>/<DATABASE>?sslmode=require
#
# THIS MUST POINT AT A DIFFERENT DATABASE FROM DATABASE_URL.
#
# The ERP simulates a separate application, bought or built independently of
# the CRM. Two such systems never share a database, and the whole point of the
# exercise is that the two datasets cannot be joined with a simple SQL query:
# the CRM knows ADV012 and BR001, the ERP knows EMP-0047 and FR-PAR-01, and
# reconciling them is deliberately left as a data-platform problem.
#
# Pointing both variables at the same database would silently destroy that.
# Nothing in the code would break - which is exactly why the warning belongs
# here rather than in a runtime check.
#
# Read by ERP/config.py only. The ERP never reads DATABASE_URL, holds no
# foreign key towards a CRM table, and imports nothing from the CRM package.
#
# For local schema work, a throwaway container is enough and needs no TLS
# (ERP/config.py leaves loopback connections alone):
#
#     docker run -d --name orialis-erp-pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=... \
#         -e POSTGRES_DB=orialis_erp -p 5433:5432 postgres:16
#     ERP_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:...@127.0.0.1:5433/orialis_erp

ERP_DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ERP_API_KEY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Shared secret protecting every /api/v1 route of the ERP API. Callers send it
# in the `X-API-Key` header; requests without it, or with a wrong one, get 401.
#
# The API refuses to start if this is not set: an API that silently runs with
# authentication disabled is worse than one that will not boot.
#
# IT MUST BE DIFFERENT FROM CRM_API_KEY.
#
# The two systems are two products. A single key opening both would make every
# CRM consumer an ERP consumer automatically, and revoking access to one would
# revoke it to the other - exactly the coupling this project exists to avoid.
#
# Generate one with:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#
# Server-side only. This value must never be prefixed with VITE_, never be
# read by anything that runs in a browser, and never be committed.

ERP_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# - Leave DATABASE_URL empty here. Fill it in `.env` only.
# - Nothing reads this variable yet: the CRM still runs on the local SQLite
#   database. It will be wired in when the connection layer is migrated.
# - Add a new placeholder line to this file whenever a new variable is
#   introduced, so the required configuration stays documented.
